{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_provisioning.py:195`\n(`test_a_missing_epic_field_aborts_provisioning`) stubs `_request` so\n`/rest/api/2/field` returns a body with `Epic Name` but WITHOUT `Epic Link`, then\ncalls `harness._assert_project_capabilities(\"RBTEST\")`. The missing field is\npermanently absent under the stub, so the readiness poll can only expire — and it\nexpires against the real PRODUCTION budget.\n\nThat budget is `scripts/jira_dc_field_readiness.py:114`,\n`FIELD_READY_BUDGET_S = 120.0`, polled every `FIELD_POLL_INTERVAL_S = 1.0`\n(line 95). `await_required_fields` (line 226) loops\n`request` -> `if monotonic() >= deadline: break` -> `sleep(interval)`, so the unit\ntest performs ~120 stub reads and sleeps ~120 real seconds to learn something the\nstub decided up front. That matches the filed 120.39-121.03s.\n\nThe readiness API is already designed for this: its docstring (lines 246-249)\nstates that `budget`/`interval` default to the MODULE-LEVEL constants read at CALL\ntime, \"so a caller (or a test) that rebinds `FIELD_READY_BUDGET_S` /\n`FIELD_POLL_INTERVAL_S` on this module actually takes effect\", and line 252 adds\nthat at least one attempt is always made \"even with a zero or negative allowance —\nthe answer must come from an observation, never from arithmetic\".\n\n## Approach\n\nRebind the module-level budget to `0.0` for this one test, via `monkeypatch`, and\nassert the resulting poll count.\n\n* `monkeypatch.setattr(harness.jira_dc_field_readiness, \"FIELD_READY_BUDGET_S\", 0.0)`\n  inside the test. `monkeypatch` restores it at teardown, so no other test and no\n  production path sees the change; the committed constant stays `120.0`.\n* With a zero budget the loop is fully DETERMINISTIC rather than merely fast: one\n  attempt is made unconditionally, `monotonic() >= deadline` is then true, and the\n  loop breaks BEFORE `sleep` is ever called. So the test asserts that the stub saw\n  exactly ONE `/rest/api/2/field` read.\n\nThat single assertion is the required guard, and it is stronger than a timing\nbudget: reaching a real sleep is only possible by going round the loop again, which\nnecessarily produces a second field read and fails the count. It contains no\nwall-clock assertion.\n\n`_stub_request` (line 72) already returns its recorded `(method, path, payload)`\nlist, so the poll count is available without new plumbing.\n\nThis is the mechanism the sibling suite already uses — \n`tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_conftest.py:478-479` monkeypatches\n`FIELD_POLL_INTERVAL_S`/`FIELD_READY_BUDGET_S` on the same module for the same\nreason — so it adds no new pattern to the tree. A fake `sleep`/`monotonic` pair\nwas rejected: `await_required_fields` accepts them, but the caller under test,\n`_assert_project_capabilities`, does not forward them, so injecting would mean\nchanging a production signature to serve a test.\n\n## Scope\n\n* `tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_provisioning.py` — rebind the budget and assert the\n  poll count in `test_a_missing_epic_field_aborts_provisioning`; note why in the\n  test docstring.\n\nNo production change: `scripts/jira_dc_field_readiness.py` and\n`tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py` are read but not modified.\n\n## Testing\n\n* Happy path — the test still raises `AssertionError` naming `Epic Link`, i.e. the\n  existing assertion is untouched, and now also pins one field read.\n* Failure/edge path — the guard is proven by seeding: temporarily restoring the\n  120s budget is confirmed to make the poll-count assertion FAIL (many reads), not\n  merely run slowly. Seed reverted, not committed.\n* Isolation check — the neighbouring readiness tests and the rest of the module\n  still pass in the same session, confirming `monkeypatch` restored the constant.\n* Production-default check — `FIELD_READY_BUDGET_S` is still `120.0` in the\n  committed tree, read directly from the source.\n* Whole-suite regression — the CI pytest command is green.\n\nNo wall-clock or timing-budget assertion is added; savings go in ticket comments.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `test_a_missing_epic_field_aborts_provisioning` still raises the same\n      `AssertionError` naming `Epic Link` — verified by the unchanged\n      `pytest.raises` + substring assertion still passing.\n- [ ] The test asserts exactly one `/rest/api/2/field` read, which proves the\n      expiry path ran without reaching `sleep` — verified by the new assertion\n      passing and by the seeded-budget run failing it.\n- [ ] The test completes in under 2 seconds where it took ~120s — measured with\n      `pytest --durations` and posted as a before/after ticket comment.\n- [ ] `scripts/jira_dc_field_readiness.py` is byte-identical to `origin/main`,\n      confirmed by an empty `git diff --stat origin/main -- <that path>`, so\n      production readiness defaults are unchanged.\n- [ ] The rest of `tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_provisioning.py` and\n      `tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_conftest.py` pass in one session, showing the\n      rebind did not leak."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786604061732623001,"uuid":"c4882712-5a2b-4dcb-8e44-5bcc9699c569"}